const body = `<h1>Run-Time Analysis (Informal)</h1>

<p>
    "How fast is it?" is the wrong question, because the answer depends on the
    machine, the language, the weather. The right question is: <strong>how does
    the work GROW when the input grows?</strong> Growth survives every hardware
    upgrade. This page is the informal version: no big-O algebra, just step
    counting and shapes.
</p>

<h2>Step Counting: The Method</h2>

<ol>
    <li>Pick the operation that dominates (usually the loop body's work).</li>
    <li>Put a counter on it.</li>
    <li>Run at n = 10, 100, 1000.</li>
    <li>Look at the RATIOS, not the times.</li>
</ol>

<pre><code>long steps = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) {
    steps++;
    // the real work
}</code></pre>

<h2>The Two Shapes You Must Recognize</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Shape</th><th>Structure</th><th>n=10</th><th>n=100</th><th>n=1000</th><th>10x input means</th></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Linear</strong></td><td>one loop over n</td><td>10</td><td>100</td><td>1,000</td><td>10x work</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Quadratic</strong></td><td>loop over n CONTAINING loop over n</td><td>100</td><td>10,000</td><td>1,000,000</td><td>100x work</td></tr>
</table>

<p>The structure tells you the shape before you run anything. One loop that
visits each element once: linear. A loop inside a loop, both controlled by n:
quadratic. This is the exam's informal analysis in one sentence.</p>

<h2>Constants Fade, Shapes Remain</h2>

<pre><code>// A: 3n steps
for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) { a++; b++; c++; }

// B: n steps
for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) { a++; }</code></pre>

<p>At small n, A takes 3x longer than B. At scale, both are "linear" and the
difference stops mattering, because quadratic versions of either would be a
thousand times worse. When comparing a linear and a quadratic algorithm, the
constants are noise. When comparing two linears, constants can matter, but the
exam almost always compares across shapes.</p>

<h2>Price the Classics</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>Shape</th><th>Why</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Sum / average / max over a list</td><td>linear</td><td>each element visited once</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Linear search</td><td>linear</td><td>worst case checks every element</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Binary search (sorted data)</td><td>logarithmic</td><td>each step halves what remains</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Selection sort / insertion sort</td><td>quadratic</td><td>loop over n, each pass scans the rest</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Merge sort</td><td>linearithmic (n log n)</td><td>log n levels of halving, linear work per level</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Print a 2D grid r x c</td><td>linear in the AREA</td><td>r*c visits, each cell once</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Check all pairs in a list</td><td>quadratic</td><td>n elements times n partners</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Logarithmic, the Feeling</h2>

<p>log n counts how many times you can halve n before reaching 1. You do not
need the math. You need three anchor numbers:</p>

<table>
    <tr><th>n</th><th>log2 n (halvings)</th></tr>
    <tr><td>1,000</td><td>about 10</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1,000,000</td><td>about 20</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1,000,000,000</td><td>about 30</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Every thousand-fold increase in data costs ten more steps. That is why binary
search on a billion sorted items takes about 30 steps, and why anyone who sorts
data first is playing a different sport. 4.7a had you build the table that
proves this. The numbers stay anchored if your hands made them.</p>

<h2>Tinker Challenge: Prove a Sort Is Quadratic</h2>

<div class="tinker">
    <h3>Step-Count Selection Sort</h3>
    <p>Take your selection sort, add a comparison counter, and run it on random
    arrays of size 100, 1000, 5000. Ratio check: 1000 vs 100 should be about
    100x the comparisons. 5000 vs 1000 should be about 25x (5 squared). If your
    ratios do not match the square law, you are counting the wrong thing.</p>
    <p>Then time it too (<code>System.currentTimeMillis()</code> before and
    after). At n=5000 the sort still finishes instantly. Quadratic is not
    "slow," it is "slow AT SCALE." Push n to 50,000 and 100,000 and find where
    the wall is on this machine. That wall is why the CED cares.</p>
</div>

<h2>Bug Hunt: The Accidental Quadratic</h2>

<div class="bug-hunt">
    <h3>Inside a Linear Algorithm</h3>
    <pre><code>// remove all items below a threshold
for (int i = 0; i &lt; list.size(); i++) {
    if (list.get(i) &lt; threshold) {
        list.remove(i);    // each remove shifts everything after it
    }
}</code></pre>
    <p>This LOOKS linear (one loop) but each remove shifts up to n elements.
    Total: potentially quadratic. Worse, it skips elements (after a remove, the
    next element slides into slot i, and i++ jumps past it). Two bugs, one
    structure. Fix: iterate backwards, or build a new list of survivors. The
    backwards fix is the classic; write it and explain why direction fixes the
    skip AND tames the shifting.</p>
</div>

<h2>Socratic Checkpoint</h2>

<div class="socratic">
    <h3>Why informal? Why not just teach big-O?</h3>
    <p>Because the reasoning matters more than the notation. "Nested loops over
    the same n means the work squares" is big-O thinking with zero Greek letters.
    The CED's phrase is "informal run-time analysis": classify, compare, predict
    ratios. Notation can come later, and it will feel like naming things you
    already own.</p>
    <h3>When is quadratic the right choice?</h3>
    <p>When n is small and known to stay small (sorting a hand of 5 cards), or
    when the linear alternative is dramatically more complex and the data set
    cannot grow. Engineering is tradeoffs under constraints. "Quadratic bad" is
    a heuristic, not a law.</p>
</div>

<h2>See Also</h2>
<ul>
    <li><a href="loop-analysis.html">Loop Analysis &amp; Tracing</a> — the tracing skills underneath step counting</li>
    <li><a href="../unit-07/sorting.html">Sorting (Unit 7)</a> — the algorithms you just priced</li>
    <li><a href="../unit-10/recursive-search.html">Recursive Search (Unit 10)</a> — log n in its recursive habitat</li>
</ul>`;
